[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Name=MarkLens
GenericName=Markdown Viewer
GenericName[vi]=Trình xem Markdown
GenericName[ja]=Markdown ビューアー
Comment=A fast, lightweight, read-only Markdown viewer
Comment[vi]=Trình xem Markdown nhanh, nhẹ, chỉ đọc
Comment[ja]=軽快で読み取り専用の Markdown ビューアー
Exec=marklens %F
Icon=marklens
Terminal=false
# One main category and one additional one, which took two passes to get right
# and desktop-file-validate found both. The original Office;Viewer;TextEditor;
# Utility; had *two* main categories, so the application could appear twice in
# the menu. Pairing TextEditor with Utility, as the spec wants, would fix that
# and assert something false: MarkLens does not edit text, and a menu entry
# under Text Editors is a promise it breaks the moment somebody tries. Viewer
# with Office above it says what this is.
Categories=Office;Viewer;
Keywords=markdown;md;mdx;viewer;reader;
Keywords[vi]=markdown;md;mdx;xem;tài liệu;
Keywords[ja]=markdown;md;mdx;ビューアー;文書;

# The file is named for the application ID, not the binary, and StartupWMClass
# matches it. GTK derives the X11 WM_CLASS res_name - and the Wayland app_id -
# from g_get_prgname(), which linux/runner/my_application.cc sets to
# APPLICATION_ID (dev.poli0981.marklens). The old StartupWMClass=marklens
# therefore never matched anything, and the symptom is a second, generic
# taskbar entry instead of the window grouping under this entry.
# test/repo/product_strings_test.dart asserts the two stay equal; `xprop
# WM_CLASS` on a real session is what confirms the premise (doc 15, S3).
StartupWMClass=dev.poli0981.marklens

# %F, not %U and not %f. %F is a list of local paths, which is exactly what the
# CLI takes (`docs/11_PACKAGING_UPDATE.md`: "marklens README.md docs/ works
# identically on both OSes") and what a multi-select in a file manager hands
# over. %U would allow URLs, and MarkLens opens local files only. %f would
# allow one path, so opening five files would start five processes — the
# single-instance forwarder would collapse them into one window, but only
# after paying for five launches.
MimeType=text/markdown;text/mdx;

# text/markdown is registered by shared-mime-info on recent distributions and
# absent on older ones, which is why `marklens-mime.xml` ships beside this. On
# a system that already knows the type the extra definition is a no-op; on one
# that does not, this line would otherwise associate MarkLens with a MIME type
# nothing ever produces.
